    "stem": "Kavya writes a two-digit number as $10a+b$ and the number with its digits reversed as $10b+a$, with digits $a \\neq b$.\n\nWhich two claims correctly justify a divisibility fact with place value?",
    "item_type": "mcq",
    "options": {
      "A": "$(10a+b)-(10b+a)=9(a-b)$, so the difference of the number and its reverse is a multiple of $9$.",
      "B": "Read $a$ and $b$ as separate digit marks only, with no place-value expression for either number.",
      "C": "Because the difference factors as $9(a-b)$, both $10a+b$ and $10b+a$ leave the same remainder on division by $9$.",
      "D": "Choose the largest product among placements of $a$ and $b$, and treat that product rule as this reverse-digit test."
    },
